# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in the two values from your Supabase project.
#
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL      Project Settings -> Data API -> Project URL.
#                        Shape: https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
#                        <project-ref> is the id already in the dashboard address bar:
#                        supabase.com/dashboard/project/<project-ref>
#                        No trailing slash, no /rest/v1.
#
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY Project Settings -> API Keys. Either works:
#                        - "Legacy API keys" tab -> "anon" "public"  (long eyJ... JWT)
#                        - "API keys" tab        -> sb_publishable_...
#
# NEVER put a secret key here (sb_secret_... / service_role). Vite inlines every
# VITE_* variable into the browser bundle, and a secret key bypasses row level
# security. The anon / publishable key is public by design; the RLS policies in
# supabase/schema.sql are what protect the data.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# End-to-end tests (npm run e2e) only. These do NOT belong in .env.local --
# put them in .env.e2e.local, which is gitignored by the *.local pattern.
# The addresses are never mailed -- the accounts are created by hand in the
# dashboard.
#
# E2E_PASSWORD has NO default and npm run e2e refuses to start without it. It
# used to default to a literal committed in this repository, which published the
# real password of two live accounts. Do not put the value back in here or in
# any other tracked file -- .env.e2e.local and a CI secret are the only homes
# for it. Both accounts share the one value, so give them the same password.
#
# E2E_PASSWORD=<the password of both test accounts>
#
# These three do have defaults, so set them only if you chose other values:
# E2E_EMAIL_A=worktime-e2e-a@worktime-e2e.dev
# E2E_EMAIL_B=worktime-e2e-b@worktime-e2e.dev
# E2E_PORT=5175
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
